[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem: Given a group ring $\mathbb{Z}[G]$ where $G$ is torsion-free, and a[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbounded chain complex $M_\bullet$ of $\mathbb{Z}[G]$-modules where each $M_i$ is[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma finitely generated free $\mathbb{Z}[G]$-module. If $H_n(M_\bullet [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}[G]} \mathbb{Z}) = 0$ for all $n$, does it imply [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$H_n(M_\bullet) = 0$ for all $n$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is essentially asking about the "zero divisor" conjecture or more [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspecifically a version of the "acyclicity" question for group rings of torsion-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfree groups.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. The map $\mathbb{Z}[G] \to \mathbb{Z}$ is the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124maugmentation map $\varepsilon$. The tensor product $M_\bullet [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}[G]} \mathbb{Z}$ is the "augmentation" of the complex, which [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcan be thought of as the complex over $\mathbb{Z}$ obtained by applying $-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}[G]} \mathbb{Z}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThere's a spectral sequence here. The hyperhomology spectral sequence or the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mKünneth-type spectral sequence. Actually, let me think more carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe have the augmentation $\varepsilon: \mathbb{Z}[G] \to \mathbb{Z}$. The kernel[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis the augmentation ideal $IG$. We have a short exact sequence:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$0 \to IG \to \mathbb{Z}[G] \to \mathbb{Z} \to 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $M_\bullet \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}[G]} \mathbb{Z}$ is obtained by tensoring the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcomplex with $\mathbb{Z}$ over $\mathbb{Z}[G]$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThere's a standard spectral sequence. Since $\mathbb{Z}[G]$ is free as a $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{Z}[G]$-module (trivially), and each $M_i$ is free, we can think about [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthis.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the key tool here is the following. Consider the augmentation ideal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfiltration or use the fact that there's a spectral sequence:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$E^2_{p,q} = \text{Tor}^{\mathbb{Z}[G]}_p(H_q(M_\bullet), \mathbb{Z}) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\Rightarrow H_{p+q}(M_\bullet \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}[G]} \mathbb{Z})$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, that's not quite right either. Let me think again.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the standard spectral sequence for a tensor product of a complex with [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma module is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$E^2_{p,q} = \text{Tor}^{\mathbb{Z}[G]}_p(H_q(M_\bullet), \mathbb{Z}) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\Rightarrow H_{p+q}(M_\bullet \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}[G]} \mathbb{Z})$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the Künneth spectral sequence (or hyper-Tor spectral sequence). Since [0m
[38;2;124;124;124meach $M_i$ is free over $\mathbb{Z}[G]$, the complex $M_\bullet$ is a complex of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mprojectives, so it can serve as a "resolution-like" object for the hyper-Tor.[0m

